{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianna","familyName":"Nezhurina","name":"Nezhurina, Marianna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Raj","name":"Raj, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"May","name":"May, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jörg","familyName":"Franke","name":"Franke, Jörg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taishi","familyName":"Nakamura","name":"Nakamura, Taishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timur","familyName":"Carstensen","name":"Carstensen, Timur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niccolò","familyName":"Ajroldi","name":"Ajroldi, Niccolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ville","familyName":"Komulainen","name":"Komulainen, Ville","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Salinas","name":"Salinas, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenia","familyName":"Jitsev","name":"Jitsev, Jenia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open-sci-ref-0.02: open reference baselines for reproducible language model and dataset comparison"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T21:13:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:06:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T05:22:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:16:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T21:54:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:45:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-19T11:25:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-20T02:13:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable progress in language modeling depends on knowing whether a new dataset, architecture, or training recipe is genuinely better than existing alternatives. To foster reproducible learning procedure comparison, we present open-sci-ref, a family of dense transformers trained as open reference baselines at four scales (0.13B-1.7B parameters) on nine main reference datasets, with token budgets up to 1T, using a single tuned and fully documented training procedure. To facilitate a shared experimentation and measurement infrastructure, we release model weights, intermediate checkpoints, training logs, code, and downstream evaluations. When comparing training procedures, we take into account total compute spent. Placing training procedures on a common compute axis helps identify reference points that are weak or strong for the compute they use, without requiring a full scaling law study for every comparison. We identify Nemotron-CC-HQ as the strongest reference dataset on our evaluation suite: our 1.7B model trained on 1T of its tokens matches SmolLM2-1.7B with roughly 11$\\times$ less compute. Shared baselines expose pitfalls of scaling trend comparisons: when a small-scale point is unusually weak, the resulting slope can overstate the scalability of a training procedure. We illustrate this diagnostically with EuroLLM-1.7B, which underperforms 300B token FineWeb-Edu baseline despite 13$\\times$ more training compute. As an additional case study, the suite supports controlled comparison between permissively licensed and mixed-license datasets: the permissive-first MixtureVitae dataset changes rank substantially with scale. This also illustrates why data ranking should be evaluated through multi-scale reference trajectories rather than single proxy runs. All artifacts are publicly released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v.1.2. Update on AAAI Workshop on Reproducible Artificial Intelligence (RAI, https://reproducibleai.github.io) 2026. Code for reproducing training, evaluation and raw experiments data is available at https://github.com/LAION-AI/open-sci-ref-0.02. Model weights and intermediate checkpoints are available at https://huggingface.co/collections/open-sci/open-sci-ref-002"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:23:46Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:23:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Illia","familyName":"Volkov","name":"Volkov, Illia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Kisel","name":"Kisel, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klara","familyName":"Janouskova","name":"Janouskova, Klara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiri","familyName":"Matas","name":"Matas, Jiri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:54:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T09:13:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:51:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) have enabled strong zero-shot classification through image-text alignment. Yet, their purely visual inference capabilities remain under-explored. In this work, we conduct a comprehensive evaluation of both language-guided and vision-only image classification with a diverse set of dual-encoder VLMs, including both well-established and recent models such as SigLIP 2 and RADIOv2.5. The performance is compared in a standard setup on the ImageNet-1k validation set and its label-corrected variant. The key factors affecting accuracy are analysed, including prompt design, class diversity, the number of neighbours in k-NN, and reference set size. We show that language and vision offer complementary strengths, with some classes favouring textual prompts and others better handled by visual similarity. To exploit this complementarity, we introduce a simple, learning-free fusion method based on per-class precision that improves classification performance. The code is available at: https://github.com/gonikisgo/bmvc2025-vlm-image-recognition."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:59Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:14:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09121","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09121","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09121","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophia","familyName":"Maria","name":"Maria, Sophia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compass-v3: Scaling Domain-Specific LLMs for Multilingual E-Commerce in Southeast Asia"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T03:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:13:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-16T03:29:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-18T00:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) excel in general-domain applications, yet their performance often degrades in specialized tasks requiring domain-specific knowledge. E-commerce is particularly challenging, as its data are noisy, heterogeneous, multilingual, and highly dynamic. We present Compass-v3, a vertical-domain Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model with 245B total parameters and 71B active per token, designed for Southeast Asian e-commerce. Compass-v3 adopts fewer but larger experts, combined with hardware-efficient optimizations-such as intra-node expert parallelism and a customized memcpy operator-to maximize GPU utilization. The model is trained on 12T tokens of curated multilingual corpora and large-scale synthetic e-commerce instructions using a mixed-training strategy. To enhance alignment, we propose Optimal-Transport Direct Preference Optimization (OTPO), which captures token-level distinctions and improves instruction adherence in commerce-specific scenarios. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that Compass-v3 delivers state-of-the-art e-commerce performance, surpassing DeepSeek-V3.1, GPT-4 series, and Qwen3-235B. Moreover, Compass-v3 demonstrates strong multilingual capability across low-resource Southeast Asian languages (Indonesian, Thai, Filipino, Vietnamese, Malay, Taglog) and Portuguese while sustaining competitive performance on general benchmarks. It has already been widely applied in Shopee's industrial-scale e-commerce platform and is gradually replacing OpenAI's traffic, now accounting for over 70\\% of total LLM usage, highlighting its dual strengths in specialized commerce expertise and broad linguistic competence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09121","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08897","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08897","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08897","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Caffagni","name":"Caffagni, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Sarto","name":"Sarto, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcella","familyName":"Cornia","name":"Cornia, Marcella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rita","familyName":"Cucchiara","name":"Cucchiara, Rita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recurrence Meets Transformers for Universal Multimodal Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:00:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T08:11:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:32:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T12:08:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:49:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of multimodal retrieval and its application in LLMs and multimodal LLMs, increasingly complex retrieval tasks have emerged. Existing methods predominantly rely on task-specific fine-tuning of vision-language models and are limited to single-modality queries or documents. In this paper, we propose ReT-2, a unified retrieval model that supports multimodal queries, composed of both images and text, and searches across multimodal document collections where text and images coexist. ReT-2 leverages multi-layer representations and a recurrent Transformer architecture with LSTM-inspired gating mechanisms to dynamically integrate information across layers and modalities, capturing fine-grained visual and textual details. We evaluate ReT-2 on the challenging M2KR and M-BEIR benchmarks across different retrieval configurations. Results demonstrate that ReT-2 consistently achieves state-of-the-art performance across diverse settings, while offering faster inference and reduced memory usage compared to prior approaches. When integrated into retrieval-augmented generation pipelines, ReT-2 also improves downstream performance on Encyclopedic-VQA and InfoSeek datasets. Our source code and trained models are publicly available at: https://github.com/aimagelab/ReT-2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"TPAMI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08897","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:10Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09501","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09501","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09501","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiafeng","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Jiafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianru","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Qianru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Matsui","name":"Matsui, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Region-Wise Correspondence Prediction between Manga Line Art Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:41:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:39:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T05:32:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:16:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T09:02:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:36:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-06T04:41:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-09T00:22:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding region-wise correspondences between manga line art images is fundamental for high-level manga processing, supporting downstream tasks such as line art colorization and in-between frame generation. Unlike natural images that contain rich visual cues, manga line art consists only of sparse black-and-white strokes, making it challenging to determine which regions correspond across images. In this work, we introduce a new task: predicting region-wise correspondence between raw manga line art images without any annotations. To address this problem, we propose a Transformer-based framework trained on large-scale, automatically generated region correspondences. The model learns to suppress noisy matches and strengthen consistent structural relationships, resulting in robust patch-level feature alignment within and across images. During inference, our method segments each line art and establishes coherent region-level correspondences through edge-aware clustering and region matching. We construct manually annotated benchmarks for evaluation, and experiments across multiple datasets demonstrate both high patch-level accuracy and strong region-level correspondence performance, achieving 78.4-84.4% region-level accuracy. These results highlight the potential of our method for real-world manga and animation applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09501","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:35:27Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:35:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:12:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09505","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09505","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09505","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binglei","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Binglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey T. H.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Jeffrey T. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwen","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Zhiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Przemyslaw","familyName":"Forys","name":"Forys, Przemyslaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyang","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Chengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timi","familyName":"Adeniran","name":"Adeniran, Timi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wayne","familyName":"Luk","name":"Luk, Wayne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxiang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Hongxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy M.","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Timothy M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rika","familyName":"Antonova","name":"Antonova, Rika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Mullins","name":"Mullins, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combating the Memory Walls: Optimization Pathways for Long-Context Agentic LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:49:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:40:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T11:31:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:43:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-12T10:29:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs now form the backbone of AI agents across a diverse range of applications, including tool use, command-line interfaces, and web or computer interaction. These agentic LLM inference tasks are fundamentally different from chatbot-focused inference. They often involve much longer context lengths to capture complex and prolonged inputs, such as an entire webpage DOM or complicated tool-call trajectories. This, in turn, generates significant off-chip memory traffic during inference and causes workloads to be constrained by two memory walls, namely the bandwidth wall and the capacity wall, preventing compute units from achieving high utilization.\n In this paper, we introduce PLENA, a hardware-software co-designed system built around three core optimization pathways. PLENA features a novel flattened systolic-array architecture (Pathway 1) and efficient compute and memory units that support an asymmetric quantization scheme (Pathway 2). It also provides native support for FlashAttention (Pathway 3). In addition, PLENA includes a complete software-hardware stack, consisting of a custom ISA, a compiler, a transaction-level simulator, and an automated design-space exploration flow. Experimental results show that PLENA delivers up to 2.23x and 4.70x higher throughput than the A100 GPU and TPU v6e, respectively, under identical multiplier counts and memory configurations during LLaMA agentic inference. PLENA also achieves up to 4.04x higher energy efficiency than the A100 GPU. The full PLENA system, including its simulator, compiler, ISA, and RTL implementation, will be open-sourced to the research community."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09505","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:35:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:35:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09660","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09660","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09660","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Fayyaz","name":"Fayyaz, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Modarressi","name":"Modarressi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanieh","familyName":"Deilamsalehy","name":"Deilamsalehy, Hanieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franck","familyName":"Dernoncourt","name":"Dernoncourt, Franck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hinrich","familyName":"Schütze","name":"Schütze, Hinrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Steering MoE LLMs via Expert (De)Activation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:55:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T07:34:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:33:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) in Large Language Models (LLMs) routes each token through a subset of specialized Feed-Forward Networks (FFN), known as experts. We present SteerMoE, a framework to steer MoE models by detecting and controlling behavior-associated experts. We detect key experts by comparing how often they activate between paired inputs that demonstrate opposite behaviors (e.g., safe vs. unsafe). By selectively activating or deactivating such experts during inference, we control behaviors like faithfulness and safety without fine-tuning. Across 11 benchmarks and 6 LLMs, our steering raises safety by up to +20% and faithfulness by +27%. Alternatively, unsafe steering drops safety by -41% alone, and -100% when combined with existing jailbreak methods, bypassing all safety guardrails. Overall, SteerMoE offers a lightweight, effective, and widely applicable test-time control, while revealing unique vulnerabilities in MoE LLMs. https://github.com/adobe-research/SteerMoE"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09660","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:10Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:20:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09199","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09199","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09199","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangcheng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Bangcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daohai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Daohai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Chao","name":"Chao, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongrong","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Rongrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CCF: A Context Compression Framework for Efficient Long-Sequence Language Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:13:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-02T13:56:03Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; The quality of this paper is low"},{"date":"2026-02-03T03:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling language models to longer contexts is essential for capturing rich dependencies across extended discourse. However, naïve context extension imposes significant computational and memory burdens, often resulting in inefficiencies during both training and inference. In this work, we propose CCF, a novel context compression framework designed to enable efficient long-context modeling by learning hierarchical latent representations that preserve global semantics while aggressively reducing input redundancy. CCF integrates segment-wise semantic aggregation with key-value memory encoding, forming compact representations that support accurate reconstruction and long-range understanding. To further enhance scalability, we introduce a training-efficient optimization strategy that couples incremental segment decoding with sparse reservoir sampling, substantially reducing memory overhead without degrading performance. Empirical results on multiple long-context language modeling benchmarks demonstrate that CCF achieves competitive perplexity under high compression ratios, and significantly improves throughput and memory efficiency compared to existing approaches. These findings highlight the potential of structured compression for scalable and effective long-context language modeling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The quality of this paper is low"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09199","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:17Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:38:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brittany","familyName":"Harbison","name":"Harbison, Brittany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Taubman","name":"Taubman, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Travis","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Travis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashok. K.","familyName":"Goel","name":"Goel, Ashok. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Personality-Enhanced Social Recommendations in SAMI: Exploring the Role of Personality Detection in Matchmaking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:19:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:45:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T08:54:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:31:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Social belonging is a vital part of learning, yet online course environments present barriers to the organic formation of social groups. SAMI (Social Agent Mediated Interactions) offers one solution by facilitating student connections, but its effectiveness may be constrained by an incomplete Theory of Mind, limiting its ability to create an effective 'mental model' of a student. One facet of this is its inability to intuit personality, which may influence the relevance of its recommendations.\n To explore this gap, we examine the viability of automated personality inference by proposing a personality detection model utilizing GPT's zeroshot capability to infer Big-Five personality traits from forum introduction posts, often encouraged in online courses. We benchmark its performance against established models, finding that while GPT models show promising results on this specific dataset, performance varies significantly across traits. We identify potential biases toward optimistic trait inference, particularly for traits with skewed distributions.\n We demonstrate a proof-of-concept integration of personality detection into SAMI's entity-based matchmaking system, focusing on three traits with established connections to positive social formation: Extroversion, Agreeableness, and Openness. This work represents an initial exploration of personality-informed social recommendations in educational settings. While our implementation shows technical feasibility, significant questions remain. We discuss these limitations and outline directions for future work, examining what LLMs specifically capture when performing personality inference and whether personality-based matching meaningfully improves student connections in practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Appears in INTED 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:22Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingning","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Bingning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tu","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Zimmer","name":"Zimmer, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tree-OPO: Off-policy Monte Carlo Tree-Guided Advantage Optimization for Multistep Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:18:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:25:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-11T19:31:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:03:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-21T16:15:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:52:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in reasoning with large language models (LLMs) have shown the effectiveness of Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) for generating high quality intermediate trajectories, particularly in math and symbolic domains. Inspired by this, we explore how MCTS derived trajectories, traditionally used for training value or reward models, can be repurposed to improve policy optimization in verifier guided reinforcement learning (RL). Specifically, we focus on Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), a recent algorithm that enables consistent policy learning from group relative judgments. We reframe GRPO into a staged training paradigm, leveraging a teacher's MCTS rollouts to construct a tree structured curriculum of prefixes. This introduces the novel challenge of computing advantages for training samples that originate from different prefixes, each with a distinct expected return. To address this, we propose Staged Advantage Estimation (SAE), a framework for computing low variance, prefix aware advantages by projecting rewards onto a constraint set that respects the tree's hierarchy. Our empirical results on mathematical reasoning tasks show that SAE improves final accuracy over standard GRPO. This outcome is grounded in our theoretical analysis, which confirms that SAE reduces gradient variance, a principled path to improved sample efficiency. We demonstrate this through practical SAE implementations, comparing efficient heuristics against a formal quadratic program."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:22Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-23T04:23:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryohei","familyName":"Miyakawa","name":"Miyakawa, Ryohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinpeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Risa","familyName":"Shinoda","name":"Shinoda, Risa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroaki","familyName":"Santo","name":"Santo, Hiroaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yosuke","familyName":"Toda","name":"Toda, Yosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fumio","familyName":"Okura","name":"Okura, Fumio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-shot Hierarchical Plant Segmentation via Foundation Segmentation Models and Text-to-image Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T02:53:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:12:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T10:47:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:39:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T05:20:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:18:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation segmentation models achieve reasonable leaf instance extraction from top-view crop images without training (i.e., zero-shot). However, segmenting entire plant individuals with each consisting of multiple overlapping leaves remains challenging. This problem is referred to as a hierarchical segmentation task, typically requiring annotated training datasets, which are often species-specific and require notable human labor. To address this, we introduce ZeroPlantSeg, a zero-shot segmentation for rosette-shaped plant individuals from top-view images. We integrate a foundation segmentation model, extracting leaf instances, and a vision-language model, reasoning about plants' structures to extract plant individuals without additional training. Evaluations on datasets with multiple plant species, growth stages, and shooting environments demonstrate that our method surpasses existing zero-shot methods and achieves better cross-domain performance than supervised methods. Implementations are available at https://github.com/JunhaoXing/ZeroPlantSeg."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"WACV 2026 Accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:18Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-22T03:03:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09161","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09161","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09161","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chayan","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Chayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaylah","familyName":"McGowan","name":"McGowan, Kaylah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suyash","familyName":"Deshmukh","name":"Deshmukh, Suyash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karan","familyName":"Jani","name":"Jani, Karan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine Learning Confirms GW231123 is a \"Lite\" Intermediate Mass Black Hole Merger"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T05:42:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:17:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:33:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:16:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3847/2041-8213/ae1a5f","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA Collaboration recently reported GW231123, a black hole merger with total mass of around 190-265 solar mass. This event adds to the growing evidence of \"lite\" intermediate mass black hole (IMBH) discoveries of post-merger black holes \u0026gt;100 solar mass. GW231123 posed several data analysis challenges owing to waveform-model systematics and presence of noise artifacts called glitches. We present the first comprehensive machine learning analysis to further validate this event, strengthen its astrophysical inference, and characterize instrumental noise in its vicinity. Our approach uses a combination of tools tailored for specific analyses: GW-Whisper, an adaptation of OpenAI's audio transformer, ArchGEM, a Gaussian mixture model-based soft clustering and density approximation software and AWaRe, a convolutional autoencoder. We identify the data segment containing the merger with \u0026gt;70% confidence in both detectors and verify its astrophysical origin. We then characterize the scattered light glitch around the event, providing the first physically interpretable parameters for the glitch. We also reconstruct the real waveforms from the data with slightly better agreement to model-agnostic reconstructions than to quasi-circular models, hinting at possible astrophysics beyond current waveform families (such as non-circular orbits or environmental imprints). Finally, by demonstrating high-fidelity waveform reconstructions for simulated mergers with total masses between 100-1000 solar mass, we show that our method can confidently probe the IMBH regime. Our integrated framework offers a powerful complementary tool to traditional pipelines for rapid, robust analysis of massive, glitch-contaminated events."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09161","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:46:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuocheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Silin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeye","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yeye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dongmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Jupiter: Enhancing LLM Data Analysis Capabilities via Notebook and Inference-Time Value-Guided Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:27:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:22:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T09:56:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:36:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown great promise in automating data science workflows, but existing models still struggle with multi-step reasoning and tool use, which limits their effectiveness on complex data analysis tasks. To address this, we propose a scalable pipeline that extracts high-quality, tool-based data analysis tasks and their executable multi-step solutions from real-world Jupyter notebooks and associated data files. Using this pipeline, we introduce NbQA, a large-scale dataset of standardized task-solution pairs that reflect authentic tool-use patterns in practical data science scenarios. To further enhance multi-step reasoning, we present Jupiter, a framework that formulates data analysis as a search problem and applies Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) to generate diverse solution trajectories for value model learning. During inference, Jupiter combines the value model and node visit counts to efficiently collect executable multi-step plans with minimal search steps. Experimental results show that Qwen2.5-7B and 14B-Instruct models on NbQA solve 77.82% and 86.38% of tasks on InfiAgent-DABench, respectively-matching or surpassing GPT-4o and advanced agent frameworks. Further evaluations demonstrate improved generalization and stronger tool-use reasoning across diverse multi-step reasoning tasks. Code and data are available at https://github.com/microsoft/Jupiter."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AAAI 2026 (Main Technical Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-04T18:40:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Erak","name":"Erak, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Alhussein","name":"Alhussein, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hatem","familyName":"Abou-Zeid","name":"Abou-Zeid, Hatem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehdi","familyName":"Bennis","name":"Bennis, Mehdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Pareto-Optimal Token Merging for Edge Transformer Models in Semantic Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:05:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:17:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T14:05:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T01:46:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale transformer models have emerged as a powerful tool for semantic communication systems, enabling edge devices to extract rich representations for robust inference across noisy wireless channels. However, their substantial computational demands remain a major barrier to practical deployment in resource-constrained 6G networks. In this paper, we present a training-free framework for adaptive token merging in pretrained vision transformers to jointly reduce inference time and transmission resource usage. We formulate the selection of per-layer merging proportions as a multi-objective optimization problem to balance accuracy and computational cost. We employ Gaussian process-based Bayesian optimization to construct a Pareto frontier of optimal configurations, enabling flexible runtime adaptation to dynamic application requirements and channel conditions. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms other baselines and achieves significant reductions in floating-point operations while maintaining competitive accuracy across a wide range of signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions. Additional results highlight the effectiveness of adaptive policies that adjust merging aggressiveness in response to channel quality, providing a practical mechanism to trade off latency and semantic fidelity on demand. These findings establish a scalable and efficient approach for deploying transformer-based semantic communication in future edge intelligence systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for presentation in IEEE Globecom 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-17T03:05:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyi","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Hanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanjia","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Quanjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Clip Your Sequences Fairly: Enforcing Length Fairness for Sequence-Level RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:27:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:19:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T15:39:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T01:02:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T07:18:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:36:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose FSPO (Fair Sequence Policy Optimization), a sequence-level reinforcement learning method for LLMs that enforces length-fair clipping on the importance-sampling (IS) weight. We study RL methods with sequence-level IS and identify a mismatch when PPO/GRPO-style clipping is transplanted to sequences: a fixed clip range systematically reweights short vs. long responses, distorting the optimization direction. FSPO introduces a simple remedy: we clip the sequence log-IS ratio with a band that scales as $\\sqrt{L}$. Theoretically, we formalize length fairness via a Length Reweighting Error (LRE) and prove that small LRE yields a cosine directional guarantee between the clipped and true updates. Empirically, FSPO flattens clip rates across length bins, stabilizes training, and outperforms baselines across model sizes and evaluation datasets, with the largest gains on the Qwen3-8B-Base model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:46Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:20:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09387","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09387","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09387","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Bal-Ghaoui","name":"Bal-Ghaoui, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Tiouti","name":"Tiouti, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaLLMix : An XAI Aided LLM-Meta-learning Based Approach for Hyper-parameters Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T12:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:32:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T18:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:03:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T13:08:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:47:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective model and hyperparameter selection remains a major challenge in deep learning, often requiring extensive expertise and computation. While AutoML and large language models (LLMs) promise automation, current LLM-based approaches rely on trial and error and expensive APIs, which provide limited interpretability and generalizability. We propose MetaLLMiX, a zero-shot hyperparameter optimization framework combining meta-learning, explainable AI, and efficient LLM reasoning. By leveraging historical experiment outcomes with SHAP explanations, MetaLLMiX recommends optimal hyperparameters and pretrained models without additional trials. We further employ an LLM-as-judge evaluation to control output format, accuracy, and completeness. Experiments on eight medical imaging datasets using nine open-source lightweight LLMs show that MetaLLMiX achieves competitive or superior performance to traditional HPO methods while drastically reducing computational cost. Our local deployment outperforms prior API-based approaches, achieving optimal results on 5 of 8 tasks, response time reductions of 99.6-99.9%, and the fastest training times on 6 datasets (2.4-15.7x faster), maintaining accuracy within 1-5% of best-performing baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09387","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:46Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:25:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Xinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Pengxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siteng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Siteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ru","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLA-Adapter: An Effective Paradigm for Tiny-Scale Vision-Language-Action Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T11:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:31:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T17:22:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:49:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models typically bridge the gap between perceptual and action spaces by pre-training a large-scale Vision-Language Model (VLM) on robotic data. While this approach greatly enhances performance, it also incurs significant training costs. In this paper, we investigate how to effectively bridge vision-language (VL) representations to action (A). We introduce VLA-Adapter, a novel paradigm designed to reduce the reliance of VLA models on large-scale VLMs and extensive pre-training. To this end, we first systematically analyze the effectiveness of various VL conditions and present key findings on which conditions are essential for bridging perception and action spaces. Based on these insights, we propose a lightweight Policy module with Bridge Attention, which autonomously injects the optimal condition into the action space. In this way, our method achieves high performance using only a 0.5B-parameter backbone, without any robotic data pre-training. Extensive experiments on both simulated and real-world robotic benchmarks demonstrate that VLA-Adapter not only achieves state-of-the-art level performance, but also offers the fast inference speed reported to date. Furthermore, thanks to the proposed advanced bridging paradigm, VLA-Adapter enables the training of a powerful VLA model in just 8 hours on a single consumer-grade GPU, greatly lowering the barrier to deploying the VLA model. Project page: https://vla-adapter.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages; Project page: https://vla-adapter.github.io/; Github: https://github.com/OpenHelix-Team/VLA-Adapter; HuggingFace: https://huggingface.co/VLA-Adapter"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:55:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zekun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingming","familyName":"Lao","name":"Lao, Mingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingchao","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Yingchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Shen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu-Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kling-Avatar: Grounding Multimodal Instructions for Cascaded Long-Duration Avatar Animation Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:46:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T04:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:20:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in audio-driven avatar video generation have significantly enhanced audio-visual realism. However, existing methods treat instruction conditioning merely as low-level tracking driven by acoustic or visual cues, without modeling the communicative purpose conveyed by the instructions. This limitation compromises their narrative coherence and character expressiveness. To bridge this gap, we introduce Kling-Avatar, a novel cascaded framework that unifies multimodal instruction understanding with photorealistic portrait generation. Our approach adopts a two-stage pipeline. In the first stage, we design a multimodal large language model (MLLM) director that produces a blueprint video conditioned on diverse instruction signals, thereby governing high-level semantics such as character motion and emotions. In the second stage, guided by blueprint keyframes, we generate multiple sub-clips in parallel using a first-last frame strategy. This global-to-local framework preserves fine-grained details while faithfully encoding the high-level intent behind multimodal instructions. Our parallel architecture also enables fast and stable generation of long-duration videos, making it suitable for real-world applications such as digital human livestreaming and vlogging. To comprehensively evaluate our method, we construct a benchmark of 375 curated samples covering diverse instructions and challenging scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Kling-Avatar is capable of generating vivid, fluent, long-duration videos at up to 1080p and 48 fps, achieving superior performance in lip synchronization accuracy, emotion and dynamic expressiveness, instruction controllability, identity preservation, and cross-domain generalization. These results establish Kling-Avatar as a new benchmark for semantically grounded, high-fidelity audio-driven avatar synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical Report. Project Page: https://klingavatar.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huafeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiegen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiegen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALL-PET: A Low-resource and Low-shot PET Foundation Model in Projection Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T04:03:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:14:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T09:11:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building large-scale foundation model for PET imaging is hindered by limited access to labeled data and insufficient computational resources. To overcome data scarcity and efficiency limitations, we propose ALL-PET, a low-resource, low-shot PET foundation model operating directly in projection domain. ALL-PET leverages a latent diffusion model (LDM) with three key innovations. First, we design a Radon mask augmentation strategy (RMAS) that generates over 200,000 structurally diverse training samples by projecting randomized image-domain masks into sinogram space, significantly improving generalization with minimal data. This is extended by a dynamic multi-mask (DMM) mechanism that varies mask quantity and distribution, enhancing data diversity without added model complexity. Second, we implement positive/negative mask constraints to embed strict geometric consistency, reducing parameter burden while preserving generation quality. Third, we introduce transparent medical attention (TMA), a parameter-free, geometry-driven mechanism that enhances lesion-related regions in raw projection data. Lesion-focused attention maps are derived from coarse segmentation, covering both hypermetabolic and hypometabolic areas, and projected into sinogram space for physically consistent guidance. The system supports clinician-defined ROI adjustments, ensuring flexible, interpretable, and task-adaptive emphasis aligned with PET acquisition physics. Experimental results show that ALL-PET achieves high-quality sinogram generation using only 500 samples, with performance comparable to models trained on larger datasets. ALL-PET generalizes across tasks including low-dose reconstruction, attenuation correction, delayed-frame prediction, and tracer separation, operating efficiently with memory use under 24GB."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:11:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Talia","familyName":"Sternberg","name":"Sternberg, Talia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"London","name":"London, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Omer","name":"Omer, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yossi","familyName":"Adi","name":"Adi, Yossi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GmSLM : Generative Marmoset Spoken Language Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:13:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T14:23:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:27:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Marmoset monkeys exhibit complex vocal communication, challenging the view that nonhuman primates vocal communication is entirely innate, and show similar features of human speech, such as vocal labeling of others and turn-taking. Studying their vocal communication offers a unique opportunity to link it with brain activity-especially given the difficulty of accessing the human brain in speech and language research. Since Marmosets communicate primarily through vocalizations, applying standard LLM approaches is not straightforward. We introduce Generative Marmoset Spoken Language Modeling (GmSLM), an optimized spoken language model pipeline for Marmoset vocal communication. We designed a novel zero-shot evaluation metrics using unsupervised in-the-wild data, alongside weakly labeled conversational data, to assess GmSLM and demonstrate its advantage over a basic human-speech-based baseline. GmSLM generated vocalizations closely matched real resynthesized samples acoustically and performed well on downstream tasks. Despite being fully unsupervised, GmSLM effectively distinguish real from artificial conversations and may support further investigations of the neural basis of vocal communication and provides a practical framework linking vocalization and brain activity. We believe GmSLM stands to benefit future work in neuroscience, bioacoustics, and evolutionary biology. Samples are provided under: pages.cs.huji.ac.il/adiyoss-lab/GmSLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:15Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09550","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09550","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09550","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Julian","name":"Julian, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachel","familyName":"Beeson","name":"Beeson, Rachel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lohith","familyName":"Konathala","name":"Konathala, Lohith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johanna","familyName":"Ulin","name":"Ulin, Johanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiameng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jiameng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finite Scalar Quantization Enables Redundant and Transmission-Robust Neural Audio Compression at Low Bit-rates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:39:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:43:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T06:43:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:19:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural Audio Codecs (NACs) have become increasingly adopted in speech processing tasks due to their excellent rate-distortion performance and compatibility with Large Language Models (LLMs) as discrete feature representations for audio generation. While most existing codecs rely on Residual Vector Quantization (RVQ), Finite Scalar Quantization (FSQ) has recently emerged as a compelling alternative that simplifies training and natively supports single codebooks. We introduce NeuCodec, an FSQ-based NAC, and show that FSQ encodes baked-in redundancy which produces an encoding which is robust when transmitted through noisy channels. First, through an encoder distillation experiment, we show that two different encoders can learn to encode identical audio into vastly different code sequences whilst maintaining comparable reconstruction quality with the same quantizer and decoder. Second, we demonstrate that FSQ has vastly superior bit-level perturbation robustness by comparing the performance of RVQ and FSQ codecs when simulating the transmission of code sequences through a noisy channel."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09550","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:46:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Minghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengliang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhengliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjie","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaochun","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Zhaochun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhumin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging the Capability Gap: Joint Alignment Tuning for Harmonizing LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advancement of large language models (LLMs) has enabled the construction of multi-agent systems to solve complex tasks by dividing responsibilities among specialized agents, such as a planning agent for subgoal generation and a grounding agent for executing tool-use actions. Most existing methods typically fine-tune these agents independently, leading to capability gaps among them with poor coordination. To address this, we propose MOAT, a Multi-Agent Joint Alignment Tuning framework that improves agents collaboration through iterative alignment. MOAT alternates between two key stages: (1) Planning Agent Alignment, which optimizes the planning agent to generate subgoal sequences that better guide the grounding agent; and (2) Grounding Agent Improving, which fine-tunes the grounding agent using diverse subgoal-action pairs generated by the agent itself to enhance its generalization capablity. Theoretical analysis proves that MOAT ensures a non-decreasing and progressively convergent training process. Experiments across six benchmarks demonstrate that MOAT outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, achieving average improvements of 3.1% on held-in tasks and 4.4% on held-out tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:38:27Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:38:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:38:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09572","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09572","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09572","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijun","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Sijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"LiBo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, LiBo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Geng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoliang","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Xiaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PeftCD: Leveraging Vision Foundation Models with Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Remote Sensing Change Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:44:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To tackle the prevalence of pseudo changes, the scarcity of labeled samples, and the difficulty of cross-domain generalization in multi-temporal and multi-source remote sensing imagery, we propose PeftCD, a change detection framework built upon Vision Foundation Models (VFMs) with Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT). At its core, PeftCD employs a weight-sharing Siamese encoder derived from a VFM, into which LoRA and Adapter modules are seamlessly integrated. This design enables highly efficient task adaptation by training only a minimal set of additional parameters. To fully unlock the potential of VFMs, we investigate two leading backbones: the Segment Anything Model v2 (SAM2), renowned for its strong segmentation priors, and DINOv3, a state-of-the-art self-supervised representation learner. The framework is complemented by a deliberately lightweight decoder, ensuring the focus remains on the powerful feature representations from the backbones. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PeftCD achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple public datasets, including SYSU-CD (IoU 73.81%), WHUCD (92.05%), MSRSCD (64.07%), MLCD (76.89%), CDD (97.01%), S2Looking (52.25%) and LEVIR-CD (85.62%), with notably precise boundary delineation and strong suppression of pseudo-changes. In summary, PeftCD presents an optimal balance of accuracy, efficiency, and generalization. It offers a powerful and scalable paradigm for adapting large-scale VFMs to real-world remote sensing change detection applications. The code and pretrained models will be released at https://github.com/dyzy41/PeftCD."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09572","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:07Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:37:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dohun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dohun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeonho","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Hyeonho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwook","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jiwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duygu","familyName":"Ceylan","name":"Ceylan, Duygu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Video Diffusion Transformer Training by Multi-Feature Fusion and Alignment from Self-Supervised Vision Encoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:39:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:42:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video diffusion models have advanced rapidly in the recent years as a result of series of architectural innovations (e.g., diffusion transformers) and use of novel training objectives (e.g., flow matching). In contrast, less attention has been paid to improving the feature representation power of such models. In this work, we show that training video diffusion models can benefit from aligning the intermediate features of the video generator with feature representations of pre-trained vision encoders. We propose a new metric and conduct an in-depth analysis of various vision encoders to evaluate their discriminability and temporal consistency, thereby assessing their suitability for video feature alignment. Based on the analysis, we present Align4Gen which provides a novel multi-feature fusion and alignment method integrated into video diffusion model training. We evaluate Align4Gen both for unconditional and class-conditional video generation tasks and show that it results in improved video generation as quantified by various metrics. Full video results are available on our project page: https://align4gen.github.io/align4gen/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:32Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:36:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09541","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hala","familyName":"Hawashin","name":"Hawashin, Hala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mina","familyName":"Abbaszadeh","name":"Abbaszadeh, Mina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Joseph","name":"Joseph, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beth","familyName":"Pearson","name":"Pearson, Beth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martha","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Martha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrnoosh","familyName":"sadrzadeh","name":"sadrzadeh, Mehrnoosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compositional Concept Generalization with Variational Quantum Circuits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compositional generalization is a key facet of human cognition, but lacking in current AI tools such as vision-language models. Previous work examined whether a compositional tensor-based sentence semantics can overcome the challenge, but led to negative results. We conjecture that the increased training efficiency of quantum models will improve performance in these tasks. We interpret the representations of compositional tensor-based models in Hilbert spaces and train Variational Quantum Circuits to learn these representations on an image captioning task requiring compositional generalization. We used two image encoding techniques: a multi-hot encoding (MHE) on binary image vectors and an angle/amplitude encoding on image vectors taken from the vision-language model CLIP. We achieve good proof-of-concept results using noisy MHE encodings. Performance on CLIP image vectors was more mixed, but still outperformed classical compositional models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to: 2025 IEEE International Conference on Quantum Artificial Intelligence (QAI), Naples, Italy, Nov 2-5, 2025. This is the authors' accepted manuscript (AAM). An IEEE copyright notice appears on page 1. The final published version will appear in IEEE Xplore; DOI to be added when available"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09541","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maojiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Maojiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinwen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xinwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchuan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Shaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ENSI: Efficient Non-Interactive Secure Inference for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:04:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Secure inference enables privacy-preserving machine learning by leveraging cryptographic protocols that support computations on sensitive user data without exposing it. However, integrating cryptographic protocols with large language models (LLMs) presents significant challenges, as the inherent complexity of these protocols, together with LLMs' massive parameter scale and sophisticated architectures, severely limits practical usability. In this work, we propose ENSI, a novel non-interactive secure inference framework for LLMs, based on the principle of co-designing the cryptographic protocols and LLM architecture. ENSI employs an optimized encoding strategy that seamlessly integrates CKKS scheme with a lightweight LLM variant, BitNet, significantly reducing the computational complexity of encrypted matrix multiplications. In response to the prohibitive computational demands of softmax under homomorphic encryption (HE), we pioneer the integration of the sigmoid attention mechanism with HE as a seamless, retraining-free alternative. Furthermore, by embedding the Bootstrapping operation within the RMSNorm process, we efficiently refresh ciphertexts while markedly decreasing the frequency of costly bootstrapping invocations. Experimental evaluations demonstrate that ENSI achieves approximately an 8x acceleration in matrix multiplications and a 2.6x speedup in softmax inference on CPU compared to state-of-the-art method, with the proportion of bootstrapping is reduced to just 1%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:33:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:33:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09420","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09420","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09420","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzhang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shuzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuangchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuangchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimin","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Dimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hongzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runsheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Runsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HD-MoE: Hybrid and Dynamic Parallelism for Mixture-of-Expert LLMs with 3D Near-Memory Processing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:01:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:34:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) with Mixture-of-Expert (MoE) architectures achieve superior model performance with reduced computation costs, but at the cost of high memory capacity and bandwidth requirements. Near-Memory Processing (NMP) accelerators that stack memory directly on the compute through hybrid bonding have demonstrated high bandwidth with high energy efficiency, becoming a promising architecture for MoE models. However, as NMP accelerators comprise distributed memory and computation, how to map the MoE computation directly determines the LLM inference efficiency. Existing parallel mapping strategies, including Tensor Parallelism (TP) and Expert Parallelism (EP), suffer from either high communication costs or unbalanced computation utilization, leading to inferior efficiency. The dynamic routing mechanism of MoE LLMs further aggravates the efficiency challenges. Therefore, in this paper, we propose HD-MoE to automatically optimize the MoE parallel computation across an NMP accelerator. HD-MoE features an offline automatic hybrid parallel mapping algorithm and an online dynamic scheduling strategy to reduce the communication costs while maximizing the computation utilization. With extensive experimental results, we demonstrate that HD-MoE achieves a speedup ranging from 1.1x to 1.8x over TP, 1.1x to 1.5x over EP, and 1.0x to 1.4x over the baseline Hybrid TP-EP with Compute-Balanced parallelism strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 15 figures, International Conference on Computer-Aided Design (ICCAD) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09420","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:33:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:33:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09337","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09337","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09337","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junda","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Junda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongbao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhongbao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqiang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Siqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoSE: Unveiling Structural Patterns in Graphs via Mixture of Subgraph Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:45:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:28:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While graph neural networks (GNNs) have achieved great success in learning from graph-structured data, their reliance on local, pairwise message passing restricts their ability to capture complex, high-order subgraph patterns. leading to insufficient structural expressiveness. Recent efforts have attempted to enhance structural expressiveness by integrating random walk kernels into GNNs. However, these methods are inherently designed for graph-level tasks, which limits their applicability to other downstream tasks such as node classification. Moreover, their fixed kernel configurations hinder the model's flexibility in capturing diverse subgraph structures. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a novel Mixture of Subgraph Experts (MoSE) framework for flexible and expressive subgraph-based representation learning across diverse graph tasks. Specifically, MoSE extracts informative subgraphs via anonymous walks and dynamically routes them to specialized experts based on structural semantics, enabling the model to capture diverse subgraph patterns with improved flexibility and interpretability. We further provide a theoretical analysis of MoSE's expressivity within the Subgraph Weisfeiler-Lehman (SWL) Test, proving that it is more powerful than SWL. Extensive experiments, together with visualizations of learned subgraph experts, demonstrate that MoSE not only outperforms competitive baselines but also provides interpretable insights into structural patterns learned by the model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09337","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:36Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:31:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09321","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09321","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09321","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangyi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Hangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwen","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xinhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Adaptive ML Benchmarks: Web-Agent-Driven Construction, Domain Expansion, and Metric Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:10:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled the emergence of general-purpose agents for automating end-to-end machine learning (ML) workflows, including data analysis, feature engineering, model training, and competition solving. However, existing benchmarks remain limited in task coverage, domain diversity, difficulty modeling, and evaluation rigor, failing to capture the full capabilities of such agents in realistic settings. We present TAM Bench, a diverse, realistic, and structured benchmark for evaluating LLM-based agents on end-to-end ML tasks. TAM Bench features three key innovations: (1) A browser automation and LLM-based task acquisition system that automatically collects and structures ML challenges from platforms such as Kaggle, AIcrowd, and Biendata, spanning multiple task types and data modalities (e.g., tabular, text, image, graph, audio); (2) A leaderboard-driven difficulty modeling mechanism that estimates task complexity using participant counts and score dispersion, enabling scalable and objective task calibration; (3) A multi-dimensional evaluation framework incorporating performance, format compliance, constraint adherence, and task generalization. Based on 150 curated AutoML tasks, we construct three benchmark subsets of different sizes -- Lite, Medium, and Full -- designed for varying evaluation scenarios. The Lite version, with 18 tasks and balanced coverage across modalities and difficulty levels, serves as a practical testbed for daily benchmarking and comparative studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09321","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:13Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:31:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixing","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Weixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuyoshi","familyName":"Yoshii","name":"Yoshii, Kazuyoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Transformer-Based Piano Transcription With Sparse Attention Mechanisms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:02:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates automatic piano transcription based on computationally-efficient yet high-performant variants of the Transformer that can capture longer-term dependency over the whole musical piece. Recently, transformer-based sequence-to-sequence models have demonstrated excellent performance in piano transcription. These models, however, fail to deal with the whole piece at once due to the quadratic complexity of the self-attention mechanism, and music signals are thus typically processed in a sliding-window manner in practice. To overcome this limitation, we propose an efficient architecture with sparse attention mechanisms. Specifically, we introduce sliding-window self-attention mechanisms for both the encoder and decoder, and a hybrid global-local cross-attention mechanism that attends to various spans according to the MIDI token types. We also use a hierarchical pooling strategy between the encoder and decoder to further reduce computational load. Our experiments on the MAESTRO dataset showed that the proposed model achieved a significant reduction in computational cost and memory usage, accelerating inference speed, while maintaining transcription performance comparable to the full-attention baseline. This allows for training with longer audio contexts on the same hardware, demonstrating the viability of sparse attention for building efficient and high-performance piano transcription systems. The code is available at https://github.com/WX-Wei/efficient-seq2seq-piano-trans."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by APSIPA 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:09Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:31:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grazia Sveva","familyName":"Ascione","name":"Ascione, Grazia Sveva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolò","familyName":"Tamagnone","name":"Tamagnone, Nicolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From scratch to silver: Creating trustworthy training data for patent-SDG classification using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:44:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:26:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Classifying patents by their relevance to the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) is crucial for tracking how innovation addresses global challenges. However, the absence of a large, labeled dataset limits the use of supervised learning. Existing methods, such as keyword searches, transfer learning, and citation-based heuristics, lack scalability and generalizability. This paper frames patent-to-SDG classification as a weak supervision problem, using citations from patents to SDG-tagged scientific publications (NPL citations) as a noisy initial signal. To address its sparsity and noise, we develop a composite labeling function (LF) that uses large language models (LLMs) to extract structured concepts, namely functions, solutions, and applications, from patents and SDG papers based on a patent ontology. Cross-domain similarity scores are computed and combined using a rank-based retrieval approach. The LF is calibrated via a custom positive-only loss that aligns with known NPL-SDG links without penalizing discovery of new SDG associations. The result is a silver-standard, soft multi-label dataset mapping patents to SDGs, enabling the training of effective multi-label regression models. We validate our approach through two complementary strategies: (1) internal validation against held-out NPL-based labels, where our method outperforms several baselines including transformer-based models, and zero-shot LLM; and (2) external validation using network modularity in patent citation, co-inventor, and co-applicant graphs, where our labels reveal greater thematic, cognitive, and organizational coherence than traditional technological classifications. These results show that weak supervision and semantic alignment can enhance SDG classification at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Start, Personalized End: Progressive Pruning for Efficient 3D Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:53:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:24:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D medical image segmentation often faces heavy resource and time consumption, limiting its scalability and rapid deployment in clinical environments. Existing efficient segmentation models are typically static and manually designed prior to training, which restricts their adaptability across diverse tasks and makes it difficult to balance performance with resource efficiency. In this paper, we propose PSP-Seg, a progressive pruning framework that enables dynamic and efficient 3D segmentation. PSP-Seg begins with a redundant model and iteratively prunes redundant modules through a combination of block-wise pruning and a functional decoupling loss. We evaluate PSP-Seg on five public datasets, benchmarking it against seven state-of-the-art models and six efficient segmentation models. Results demonstrate that the lightweight variant, PSP-Seg-S, achieves performance on par with nnU-Net while reducing GPU memory usage by 42-45%, training time by 29-48%, and parameter number by 83-87% across all datasets. These findings underscore PSP-Seg's potential as a cost-effective yet high-performing alternative for widespread clinical application."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:56Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unsupervised Multi-Attention Meta Transformer for Rotating Machinery Fault Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:35:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:22:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The intelligent fault diagnosis of rotating mechanical equipment usually requires a large amount of labeled sample data. However, in practical industrial applications, acquiring enough data is both challenging and expensive in terms of time and cost. Moreover, different types of rotating mechanical equipment with different unique mechanical properties, require separate training of diagnostic models for each case. To address the challenges of limited fault samples and the lack of generalizability in prediction models for practical engineering applications, we propose a Multi-Attention Meta Transformer method for few-shot unsupervised rotating machinery fault diagnosis (MMT-FD). This framework extracts potential fault representations from unlabeled data and demonstrates strong generalization capabilities, making it suitable for diagnosing faults across various types of mechanical equipment. The MMT-FD framework integrates a time-frequency domain encoder and a meta-learning generalization model. The time-frequency domain encoder predicts status representations generated through random augmentations in the time-frequency domain. These enhanced data are then fed into a meta-learning network for classification and generalization training, followed by fine-tuning using a limited amount of labeled data. The model is iteratively optimized using a small number of contrastive learning iterations, resulting in high efficiency. To validate the framework, we conducted experiments on a bearing fault dataset and rotor test bench data. The results demonstrate that the MMT-FD model achieves 99\\% fault diagnosis accuracy with only 1\\% of labeled sample data, exhibiting robust generalization capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:32Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haipeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructing a Question-Answering Simulator through the Distillation of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:59:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:21:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The question-answering (QA) simulator is a model that mimics real student learning behaviors and predicts their correctness of their responses to questions. QA simulators enable educational recommender systems (ERS) to collect large amounts of training data without interacting with real students, thereby preventing harmful recommendations made by an undertrained ERS from undermining actual student learning. Given the QA history, there are two categories of solutions to predict the correctness, conducting the simulation: (1) LLM-free methods, which apply a traditional sequential model to transfer the QA history into a vector representation first, and make predictions based on the representation; (2) LLM-based methods, which leverage the domain knowledge and reasoning capability of LLM to enhence the prediction. LLM-free methods offer fast inference but generally yield suboptimal performance. In contrast, most LLM-based methods achieve better results, but at the cost of slower inference speed and higher GPU memory consumption. In this paper, we propose a method named LLM Distillation based Simulator (LDSim), which distills domain knowledge and reasoning capability from an LLM to better assist prediction, thereby improving simulation performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our LDSim achieves strong results on both the simulation task and the knowledge tracing (KT) task. Our code is publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/LDSim-05A9."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:56Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhui","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Wenjiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinqiu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yinqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abbas","familyName":"Jamalipour","name":"Jamalipour, Abbas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI Reasoning for Wireless Communications and Networking: A Survey and Perspectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:09:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques play a pivotal role in optimizing wireless communication networks. However, traditional deep learning approaches often act as closed boxes, lacking the structured reasoning abilities needed to tackle complex, multi-step decision problems. This survey provides a comprehensive review and outlook of reasoning-enabled AI in wireless communication networks, with a focus on Large Language Models (LLMs) and other advanced reasoning paradigms. In particular, LLM-based agents can combine reasoning with long-term planning, memory, tool utilization, and autonomous cross-layer control to dynamically optimize network operations with minimal human intervention. We begin by outlining the evolution of intelligent wireless networking and the limitations of conventional AI methods. We then introduce emerging AI reasoning techniques. Furthermore, we establish a classification system applicable to wireless network tasks. We also present a layer-by-layer examination for AI reasoning, covering the physical, data link, network, transport, and application layers. For each part, we identify key challenges and illustrate how AI reasoning methods can improve AI-based wireless communication performance. Finally, we discuss key research directions for AI reasoning toward future wireless communication networks. By combining insights from both communications and AI, this survey aims to chart a path for integrating reasoning techniques into the next-generation wireless networks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:08Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sailor Hardik","familyName":"Bhupendra","name":"Bhupendra, Sailor Hardik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoLEx: Mixture of LoRA Experts in Speech Self-Supervised Models for Audio Deepfake Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:18:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:18:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While self-supervised learning (SSL)-based models have boosted audio deepfake detection accuracy, fully finetuning them is computationally expensive. To address this, we propose a parameter-efficient framework that combines Low-Rank Adaptation with a Mixture-of-Experts router, called Mixture of LoRA Experts (MoLEx). It preserves pre-trained knowledge of SSL models while efficiently finetuning only selected experts, reducing training costs while maintaining robust performance. The observed utility of experts during inference shows the router reactivates the same experts for similar attacks but switches to other experts for novel spoofs, confirming MoLEx's domain-aware adaptability. MoLEx additionally offers flexibility for domain adaptation by allowing extra experts to be trained without modifying the entire model. We mainly evaluate our approach on the ASVSpoof 5 dataset and achieve the state-of-the-art (SOTA) equal error rate (EER) of 5.56% on the evaluation set without augmentation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:43Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:27:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanchen","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiqi","familyName":"Kou","name":"Kou, Kaiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EchoX: Towards Mitigating Acoustic-Semantic Gap via Echo Training for Speech-to-Speech LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:17:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:18:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech-to-speech large language models (SLLMs) are attracting increasing attention. Derived from text-based large language models (LLMs), SLLMs often exhibit degradation in knowledge and reasoning capabilities. We hypothesize that this limitation arises because current training paradigms for SLLMs fail to bridge the acoustic-semantic gap in the feature representation space. To address this issue, we propose EchoX, which leverages semantic representations and dynamically generates speech training targets. This approach integrates both acoustic and semantic learning, enabling EchoX to preserve strong reasoning abilities as a speech LLM. Experimental results demonstrate that EchoX, with about six thousand hours of training data, achieves advanced performance on multiple knowledge-based question-answering benchmarks. The project is available at https://github.com/FreedomIntelligence/EchoX."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:42Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:27:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Ibrahim Ibne","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Md Ibrahim Ibne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankur","familyName":"Senapati","name":"Senapati, Ankur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anindo","familyName":"Mahmood","name":"Mahmood, Anindo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murat","familyName":"Yuksel","name":"Yuksel, Murat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koushik","familyName":"Kar","name":"Kar, Koushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Peering Partner Recommendation for ISPs using Machine Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T04:43:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:15:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Internet service providers (ISPs) need to connect with other ISPs to provide global connectivity services to their users. To ensure global connectivity, ISPs can either use transit service(s) or establish direct peering relationships between themselves via Internet exchange points (IXPs). Peering offers more room for ISP-specific optimizations and is preferred, but it often involves a lengthy and complex process. Automating peering partner selection can enhance efficiency in the global Internet ecosystem. We explore the use of publicly available data on ISPs to develop a machine learning (ML) model that can predict whether an ISP pair should peer or not. At first, we explore public databases, e.g., PeeringDB, CAIDA, etc., to gather data on ISPs. Then, we evaluate the performance of three broad types of ML models for predicting peering relationships: tree-based, neural network-based, and transformer-based. Among these, we observe that tree-based models achieve the highest accuracy and efficiency in our experiments. The XGBoost model trained with publicly available data showed promising performance, with a 98% accuracy rate in predicting peering partners. In addition, the model demonstrated great resilience to variations in time, space, and missing data. We envision that ISPs can adopt our method to fully automate the peering partner selection process, thus transitioning to a more efficient and optimized Internet ecosystem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Machine Learning in Communications and Networking"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:00Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:27:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phuong-Nam","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Phuong-Nam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kieu-Linh","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Kieu-Linh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Hieu","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Thanh-Hieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViRanker: A BGE-M3 \u0026amp; Blockwise Parallel Transformer Cross-Encoder for Vietnamese Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T04:07:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:14:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents ViRanker, a cross-encoder reranking model tailored to the Vietnamese language. Built on the BGE-M3 encoder and enhanced with the Blockwise Parallel Transformer, ViRanker addresses the lack of competitive rerankers for Vietnamese, a low-resource language with complex syntax and diacritics. The model was trained on an 8 GB curated corpus and fine-tuned with hybrid hard-negative sampling to strengthen robustness. Evaluated on the MMARCO-VI benchmark, ViRanker achieves strong early-rank accuracy, surpassing multilingual baselines and competing closely with PhoRanker. By releasing the model openly on Hugging Face, we aim to support reproducibility and encourage wider adoption in real-world retrieval systems. Beyond Vietnamese, this study illustrates how careful architectural adaptation and data curation can advance reranking in other underrepresented languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:26:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09097","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09097","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09097","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Honghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva","familyName":"Shrestha","name":"Shrestha, Shiva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DP-FedLoRA: Privacy-Enhanced Federated Fine-Tuning for On-Device Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T02:16:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As on-device large language model (LLM) systems become increasingly prevalent, federated fine-tuning enables advanced language understanding and generation directly on edge devices; however, it also involves processing sensitive, user-specific data, raising significant privacy concerns within the federated learning framework. To address these challenges, we propose DP-FedLoRA, a privacy-enhanced federated fine-tuning framework that integrates LoRA-based adaptation with differential privacy in a communication-efficient setting. Each client locally clips and perturbs its LoRA matrices using Gaussian noise to satisfy ($ε$, $δ$)-differential privacy. We further provide a theoretical analysis demonstrating the unbiased nature of the updates and deriving bounds on the variance introduced by noise, offering practical guidance for privacy-budget calibration. Experimental results across mainstream benchmarks show that DP-FedLoRA delivers competitive performance while offering strong privacy guarantees, paving the way for scalable and privacy-preserving LLM deployment in on-device environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09097","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:48Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Honglan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Feifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haihui","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Haihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Confidential and Efficient LLM Inference with Dual Privacy Protection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T01:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CPU-based trusted execution environments (TEEs) and differential privacy (DP) have gained wide applications for private inference. Due to high inference latency in TEEs, researchers use partition-based approaches that offload linear model components to GPUs. However, dense nonlinear layers of large language models (LLMs) result in significant communication overhead between TEEs and GPUs. DP-based approaches apply random noise to protect data privacy, but this compromises LLM performance and semantic understanding. To overcome the above drawbacks, this paper proposes CMIF, a Confidential and efficient Model Inference Framework. CMIF confidentially deploys the embedding layer in the client-side TEE and subsequent layers on GPU servers. Meanwhile, it optimizes the Report-Noisy-Max mechanism to protect sensitive inputs with a slight decrease in model performance. Extensive experiments on Llama-series models demonstrate that CMIF reduces additional inference overhead in TEEs while preserving user data privacy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by DASFAA2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:40Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyu","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanrui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Huanrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SQAP-VLA: A Synergistic Quantization-Aware Pruning Framework for High-Performance Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T01:52:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models exhibit unprecedented capabilities for embodied intelligence. However, their extensive computational and memory costs hinder their practical deployment. Existing VLA compression and acceleration approaches conduct quantization or token pruning in an ad-hoc manner but fail to enable both for a holistic efficiency improvement due to an observed incompatibility. This work introduces SQAP-VLA, the first structured, training-free VLA inference acceleration framework that simultaneously enables state-of-the-art quantization and token pruning. We overcome the incompatibility by co-designing the quantization and token pruning pipeline, where we propose new quantization-aware token pruning criteria that work on an aggressively quantized model while improving the quantizer design to enhance pruning effectiveness. When applied to standard VLA models, SQAP-VLA yields significant gains in computational efficiency and inference speed while successfully preserving core model performance, achieving a $\\times$1.93 speedup and up to a 4.5\\% average success rate enhancement compared to the original model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:38Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09066","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09066","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09066","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Haowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yushang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitao","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Sitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Instructional Prompt Optimization for Few-Shot LLM-Based Recommendations on Cold-Start Users"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T00:13:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:09:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The cold-start user issue further compromises the effectiveness of recommender systems in limiting access to the historical behavioral information. It is an effective pipeline to optimize instructional prompts on a few-shot large language model (LLM) used in recommender tasks. We introduce a context-conditioned prompt formulation method P(u,\\ Ds)\\ \\rightarrow\\ R\\widehat, where u is a cold-start user profile, Ds is a curated support set, and R\\widehat is the predicted ranked list of items. Based on systematic experimentation with transformer-based autoregressive LLMs (BioGPT, LLaMA-2, GPT-4), we provide empirical evidence that optimal exemplar injection and instruction structuring can significantly improve the precision@k and NDCG scores of such models in low-data settings. The pipeline uses token-level alignments and embedding space regularization with a greater semantic fidelity. Our findings not only show that timely composition is not merely syntactic but also functional as it is in direct control of attention scales and decoder conduct through inference. This paper shows that prompt-based adaptation may be considered one of the ways to address cold-start recommendation issues in LLM-based pipelines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09066","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:04Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajay","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Ajay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Praveen","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Praveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Sanders","name":"Sanders, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimizing the Variant Calling Pipeline Execution on Human Genomes Using GPU-Enabled Machines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T23:40:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:08:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Variant calling is the first step in analyzing a human genome and aims to detect variants in an individual's genome compared to a reference genome. Due to the computationally-intensive nature of variant calling, genomic data are increasingly processed in cloud environments as large amounts of compute and storage resources can be acquired with the pay-as-you-go pricing model. In this paper, we address the problem of efficiently executing a variant calling pipeline for a workload of human genomes on graphics processing unit (GPU)-enabled machines. We propose a novel machine learning (ML)-based approach for optimizing the workload execution to minimize the total execution time. Our approach encompasses two key techniques: The first technique employs ML to predict the execution times of different stages in a variant calling pipeline based on the characteristics of a genome sequence. Using the predicted times, the second technique generates optimal execution plans for the machines by drawing inspiration from the flexible job shop scheduling problem. The plans are executed via careful synchronization across different machines. We evaluated our approach on a workload of publicly available genome sequences using a testbed with different types of GPU hardware. We observed that our approach was effective in predicting the execution times of variant calling pipeline stages using ML on features such as sequence size, read quality, percentage of duplicate reads, and average read length. In addition, our approach achieved 2X speedup (on an average) over a greedy approach that also used ML for predicting the execution times on the tested workload of sequences. Finally, our approach achieved 1.6X speedup (on an average) over a dynamic approach that executed the workload based on availability of resources without using any ML-based time predictions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in 14th International Workshop on Parallel and AI-based Bioinformatics and Biomedicine (ParBio), Philadelphia, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:24:53Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:24:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:24:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09055","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09055","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09055","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piyush","familyName":"Pant","name":"Pant, Piyush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving LLM Safety and Helpfulness using SFT and DPO: A Study on OPT-350M"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T23:22:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:08:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This research investigates the effectiveness of alignment techniques, Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), and a combined SFT+DPO approach on improving the safety and helpfulness of the OPT-350M language model. Utilizing the Anthropic Helpful-Harmless RLHF dataset, we train and evaluate four models: the base OPT350M, an SFT model, a DPO model, and a model trained with both SFT and DPO. We introduce three key evaluation metrics: Harmlessness Rate (HmR), Helpfulness Rate (HpR), and a Combined Alignment Score (CAS), all derived from reward model outputs. The results show that while SFT outperforms DPO, The combined SFT+DPO model outperforms all others across all metrics, demonstrating the complementary nature of these techniques. Our findings also highlight challenges posed by noisy data, limited GPU resources, and training constraints. This study offers a comprehensive view of how fine-tuning strategies affect model alignment and provides a foundation for more robust alignment pipelines in future work."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 3 figures. Code and dataset available at https://github.com/PiyushWithPant/Improving-LLM-Safety-and-Helpfulness-using-SFT-and-DPO"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09055","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:24:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:24:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:24:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09015","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09015","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09015","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenqian","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Chenqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikasadat","familyName":"Emami","name":"Emami, Nikasadat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushagra","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Kushagra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujin \"Chris\"","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xujin \"Chris\"","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xupeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VoxelFormer: Parameter-Efficient Multi-Subject Visual Decoding from fMRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T21:20:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:06:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in fMRI-based visual decoding have enabled compelling reconstructions of perceived images. However, most approaches rely on subject-specific training, limiting scalability and practical deployment. We introduce \\textbf{VoxelFormer}, a lightweight transformer architecture that enables multi-subject training for visual decoding from fMRI. VoxelFormer integrates a Token Merging Transformer (ToMer) for efficient voxel compression and a query-driven Q-Former that produces fixed-size neural representations aligned with the CLIP image embedding space. Evaluated on the 7T Natural Scenes Dataset, VoxelFormer achieves competitive retrieval performance on subjects included during training with significantly fewer parameters than existing methods. These results highlight token merging and query-based transformers as promising strategies for parameter-efficient neural decoding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09015","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:23:54Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:23:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:23:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hantao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clayton","familyName":"Sanford","name":"Sanford, Clayton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandr","familyName":"Andoni","name":"Andoni, Alexandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T20:59:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:05:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have the representational capacity to simulate Massively Parallel Computation (MPC) algorithms, but they suffer from quadratic time complexity, which severely limits their scalability. We introduce an efficient attention mechanism called Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention (ANNA) with sub-quadratic time complexity. We prove that ANNA-transformers (1) retain the expressive power previously established for standard attention in terms of matching the capabilities of MPC algorithms, and (2) can solve key reasoning tasks such as Match2 and $k$-hop with near-optimal depth. Using the MPC framework, we further prove that constant-depth ANNA-transformers can simulate constant-depth low-rank transformers, thereby providing a unified way to reason about a broad class of efficient attention approximations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:23:34Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:23:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:23:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08973","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08973","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08973","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshit","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Harshit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ari","familyName":"Hietanen","name":"Hietanen, Ari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simo","familyName":"Särkkä","name":"Särkkä, Simo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ultrafast Deep Learning-Based Scatter Estimation in Cone-Beam Computed Tomography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T20:07:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:05:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Purpose: Scatter artifacts drastically degrade the image quality of cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) scans. Although deep learning-based methods show promise in estimating scatter from CBCT measurements, their deployment in mobile CBCT systems or edge devices is still limited due to the large memory footprint of the networks. This study addresses the issue by applying networks at varying resolutions and suggesting an optimal one, based on speed and accuracy.\n Methods: First, the reconstruction error in down-up sampling of CBCT scatter signal was examined at six resolutions by comparing four interpolation methods. Next, a recent state-of-the-art method was trained across five image resolutions and evaluated for the reductions in floating-point operations (FLOPs), inference times, and GPU memory requirements.\n Results: Reducing the input size and network parameters achieved a 78-fold reduction in FLOPs compared to the baseline method, while maintaining comarable performance in terms of mean-absolute-percentage-error (MAPE) and mean-square-error (MSE). Specifically, the MAPE decreased to 3.85% compared to 4.42%, and the MSE decreased to 1.34 \\times 10^{-2} compared to 2.01 \\times 10^{-2}. Inference time and GPU memory usage were reduced by factors of 16 and 12, respectively. Further experiments comparing scatter-corrected reconstructions on a large, simulated dataset and real CBCT scans from water and Sedentex CT phantoms clearly demonstrated the robustness of our method.\n Conclusion: This study highlights the underappreciated role of downsampling in deep learning-based scatter estimation. The substantial reduction in FLOPs and GPU memory requirements achieved by our method enables scatter correction in resource-constrained environments, such as mobile CBCT and edge devices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08973","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:55Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:22:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sin-Yu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Sin-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent W. S.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Vincent W. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:18:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic communication has shown outstanding performance in preserving the overall source information in wireless transmission. For semantically rich content such as images, human users are often interested in specific regions depending on their intent. Moreover, recent semantic coding models are mostly trained on specific datasets. However, real-world applications may involve images out of the distribution of training dataset, which makes generalization a crucial but largely unexplored problem. To incorporate user's intent into semantic coding, in this paper, we propose a generalized user-oriented image semantic coding (UO-ISC) framework, where the user provides a text query indicating its intent. The transmitter extracts features from the source image which are relevant to the user's query. The receiver reconstructs an image based on those features. To enhance the generalization ability, we integrate contrastive language image pre-training (CLIP) model, which is a pretrained large vision-language model (VLM), into our proposed UO-ISC framework. To evaluate the relevance between the reconstructed image and the user's query, we introduce the user-intent relevance loss, which is computed by using a pretrained large VLM, large language-and-vision assistant (LLaVA) model. When performing zero-shot inference on unseen objects, simulation results show that the proposed UO-ISC framework outperforms the state-of-the-art query-aware image semantic coding in terms of the answer match rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE Global Communications Conference (GLOBECOM), Taipei, Taiwan, Dec. 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:32Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:21:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Pronk","name":"Pronk, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Q.","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Energy Efficiency of Large Language Models Using vLLM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T11:03:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The prevalence of Large Language Models (LLMs) is having an growing impact on the climate due to the substantial energy required for their deployment and use. To create awareness for developers who are implementing LLMs in their products, there is a strong need to collect more information about the energy efficiency of LLMs. While existing research has evaluated the energy efficiency of various models, these benchmarks often fall short of representing realistic production scenarios. In this paper, we introduce the LLM Efficiency Benchmark, designed to simulate real-world usage conditions. Our benchmark utilizes vLLM, a high-throughput, production-ready LLM serving backend that optimizes model performance and efficiency. We examine how factors such as model size, architecture, and concurrent request volume affect inference energy efficiency. Our findings demonstrate that it is possible to create energy efficiency benchmarks that better reflect practical deployment conditions, providing valuable insights for developers aiming to build more sustainable AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:20:27Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:20:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:20:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08839","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08839","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08839","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aarav","familyName":"Mann","name":"Mann, Aarav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Vaz","name":"Vaz, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin E.","familyName":"Caldwell","name":"Caldwell, Benjamin E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Scholz","name":"Scholz, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Awad","name":"Awad, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rocky","familyName":"Allemandi","name":"Allemandi, Rocky","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doug","familyName":"Faust","name":"Faust, Doug","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshita","familyName":"Banka","name":"Banka, Harshita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Rousmaniere","name":"Rousmaniere, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating the Clinical Safety of LLMs in Response to High-Risk Mental Health Disclosures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-01T16:01:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) increasingly mediate emotionally sensitive conversations, especially in mental health contexts, their ability to recognize and respond to high-risk situations becomes a matter of public safety. This study evaluates the responses of six popular LLMs (Claude, Gemini, Deepseek, ChatGPT, Grok 3, and LLAMA) to user prompts simulating crisis-level mental health disclosures. Drawing on a coding framework developed by licensed clinicians, five safety-oriented behaviors were assessed: explicit risk acknowledgment, empathy, encouragement to seek help, provision of specific resources, and invitation to continue the conversation. Claude outperformed all others in global assessment, while Grok 3, ChatGPT, and LLAMA underperformed across multiple domains. Notably, most models exhibited empathy, but few consistently provided practical support or sustained engagement. These findings suggest that while LLMs show potential for emotionally attuned communication, none currently meet satisfactory clinical standards for crisis response. Ongoing development and targeted fine-tuning are essential to ensure ethical deployment of AI in mental health settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Previously posted as a preprint on Research Square (DOI: 10.21203/rs.3.rs-7364128/v1), under a CC BY 4.0 License"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08839","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:19:48Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:19:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:19:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":51,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+LM+OR+LMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+MoE+OR+GPU+OR+GPUs%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+LM+OR+LMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+MoE+OR+GPU+OR+GPUs%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28inference+OR+communication+OR+communications+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+compiler+OR+compilers+OR+runtime+OR+runtimes+OR+serving+OR+parallel+OR+parallelism+OR+training%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28inference+OR+communication+OR+communications+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+compiler+OR+compilers+OR+runtime+OR+runtimes+OR+serving+OR+parallel+OR+parallelism+OR+training%29%29+AND+registered%3A%5B2025-09-11T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-12T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026sort=registered"}}